59,3,,status,Minako,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Minato,,Option A seems the nicest for the treasurer because they wouldn't have to chase money every month. It would give them so much more time to relax and be with us.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option A is too risky. Given that a third of members struggle with lump sums, a flat annual fee would likely increase the number of people who cannot pay, leading to more chasing and financial instability.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Minako,,Option A would likely alienate the potential members I found in the records. Requiring a full year's commitment upfront is a significant barrier for newcomers and those who prefer a more flexible arrangement.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,May,,"Option B sounds like a nightmare! If we charge for every single event, people will just keep doing that math in their heads and hesitate to come out. It's a total vibe-killer.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option B is the only one that stops the regulars from subsidizing the ghosts. If you only pay for what you use, the people who actually show up aren't paying for the eighteen people who don't.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Shunichi, but if we do Option B, won't the treasurer have to spend even more time chasing small amounts of money every single time we meet? That sounds like it would be very exhausting.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Minato, you are correct. Option B would maximize the administrative burden, requiring constant tracking of individual attendance and frequent small transactions. It is the least efficient choice for the treasurer's workload.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Shunichi, while Option B addresses the fairness of usage, it creates a high level of visibility regarding who is attending and who isn't. This could make newcomers feel scrutinized rather than welcome.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,May,,"@Minako, exactly! Imagine the awkwardness of having to pay a fee at the door every time. It turns a friendly gathering into a transaction. We want people to feel like they belong, not like they're buying a ticket to a show.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, the ""transaction"" is already happening; the regulars are just paying it in labor and resentment. @Minato, I'm sure the treasurer would prefer a bit of bookkeeping over the current state of chaos and unfairness.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May"",""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Minato,,"I don't think the treasurer would like that. Six hours a month is already so much, and Option B sounds like it would be even more. I'm worried they might want to quit again.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"Option C provides a middle ground. It allows us to secure the fixed costs through the base tiers while offering different commitment levels, which should be more manageable than Option B and less daunting than Option A.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Minako,,"Option C is a reasonable compromise. It allows newcomers to enter at a lower commitment level without the treasurer needing to perform manual calculations for every mid-year joiner, as the tiers are predefined.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, I can get behind Option C. It's not as scary as a huge lump sum, and it doesn't turn every single evening into a payment booth. It keeps the spirit of the club alive!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"Option C is a decent compromise, provided the tiers actually reflect the usage gap. If the ""low"" tier is too cheap, the ghosts still win. But it's certainly more rational than Option A.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Minato,,I think Option C is a good idea. It seems fairer than Option A and much kinder to the treasurer than Option B. I'll vote for Option C!,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,Option C is the most rational choice. It secures our high fixed costs while providing the flexibility needed to avoid the payment failures associated with Option A and the chaos of Option B. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Minako,,Option C maintains a level of discretion and ease of entry that the other options lack. It allows people to find their feet without feeling scrutinized or burdened. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,May,,I'm totally on board with Option C! It's the only way to keep the atmosphere light and welcoming without making everyone stress over their wallets every single time they want to hang out. I vote for Option C!,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"I'll go with Option C. It's not as pure a solution as B for the fairness issue, but it's the only one that doesn't completely ignore the reality of our attendance gap. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
